<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Most keys the app needs are build settings (INFOPLIST_KEY_* in the
	     project), which Xcode merges into the Info.plist it generates. This
	     file holds the three Xcode has no build setting for.

	     UIBackgroundModes `location` is the whole reason the app exists: with
	     it, and `allowsBackgroundLocationUpdates` set in Core/Recorder.swift,
	     a ride started in the app keeps recording with the screen locked
	     under the ordinary "While Using the App" permission. iOS shows the
	     blue location pill while it does, which is the honest thing.

	     UIBackgroundModes `audio` is for the voice that says the turns of the
	     way somewhere (Core/Guide.swift): a recording a second long, played
	     with the screen locked, which iOS lets an app start from the
	     background only with this. Nothing plays for a ride with nowhere to
	     go, or with the voice switched off. It is also what keeps the radio
	     (Core/Radio.swift) playing in a pocket, for a rider who turned it on.

	     CFBundleLocalizations tells iOS the app speaks Estonian, Russian and Azerbaijani as
	     well as English, so the permission prompt is asked in the phone's
	     language (the texts are in Rattatee/<lang>.lproj/InfoPlist.strings)
	     and Settings offers a per-app language.

	     It lives outside Rattatee/ because that folder is a synchronised
	     group: a plist inside it would also be copied into the bundle.

	     UILaunchScreen is what iOS draws while the app starts, before any of
	     its code has run. Xcode's generated one is empty, which iOS draws in
	     the system background: plain black on a phone set to dark. LaunchPaper
	     (Assets.xcassets) is the app's own ground as the phone is set: by
	     day the paper every colour shares (`Palette.dayPaper`), by night
	     Blue's, the colour a new install opens in, so the start is the app's
	     own ground and the map arrives on it. Somebody who chose another
	     colour and sees it by night, or fixed Light or Dark, sees the wrong
	     one for a moment: the launch screen is fixed at build time and
	     cannot know. -->
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIColorName</key>
		<string>LaunchPaper</string>
	</dict>
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>location</string>
		<string>audio</string>
	</array>
	<key>CFBundleLocalizations</key>
	<array>
		<string>en</string>
		<string>et</string>
		<string>ru</string>
		<string>az</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
